1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:05,517
Ask any AI chatbot to plan a day in San Francisco, and it will happily

2
00:00:05,617 --> 00:00:11,323
invent events, get dates wrong, or send a family with kids to a 21+ show.

3
00:00:11,423 --> 00:00:13,153
BAYBAY Crew fixes that:

4
00:00:13,253 --> 00:00:18,561
four AI agents plan a real day, where every stop comes from verified

5
00:00:18,661 --> 00:00:22,061
data, and is checked before you ever see it.

6
00:00:22,761 --> 00:00:27,945
The four agents, BAYBAY the host, Scout, Planner and Checker, coordinate

7
00:00:28,045 --> 00:00:29,781
only through a Band room.

8
00:00:29,881 --> 00:00:34,845
Every handoff is a Band message with an @mention, and the whole state

9
00:00:34,945 --> 00:00:40,204
travels inside those messages, so if you delete the room, the crew stops.

10
00:00:40,304 --> 00:00:45,121
Scout and Checker query a Neo4j knowledge graph built from BAYLINK:

11
00:00:45,221 --> 00:00:50,113
about 1,600 nodes of real, dated events, venues, neighborhoods, free

12
00:00:50,213 --> 00:00:52,535
museum days and transit stations.

13
00:00:52,635 --> 00:00:56,572
And every reasoning step runs on open models on Crusoe:

14
00:00:56,672 --> 00:01:01,637
DeepSeek V4 Flash for Scout, DeepSeek V4 Pro for Planner, and Gemma 4

15
00:01:01,737 --> 00:01:05,380
for Checker, so a different model reviews the plan.

16
00:01:06,080 --> 00:01:07,150
Let's run it live:

17
00:01:07,250 --> 00:01:11,505
a Saturday with two kids, live music and something to eat, by Muni.

18
00:01:11,605 --> 00:01:14,235
The top bar shows all three sponsors live.

19
00:01:14,335 --> 00:01:18,719
Scout turns the request into filters on Crusoe in under a second, and

20
00:01:18,819 --> 00:01:20,409
runs Cypher on Neo4j Aura.

21
00:01:20,509 --> 00:01:25,154
The graph lights up with the venues, neighborhoods and stations it found.

22
00:01:25,254 --> 00:01:26,649
Planner drafts the day.

23
00:01:26,749 --> 00:01:30,938
Then Checker re-reads every stop from the graph, a different model

24
00:01:31,038 --> 00:01:35,163
judges the fit, and here it vetoes round one and sends it back to

25
00:01:35,263 --> 00:01:37,698
Planner with concrete fixes. Round two:

26
00:01:37,798 --> 00:01:39,648
every stop verified, approved.

27
00:01:40,348 --> 00:01:42,438
The result is a verified plan:

28
00:01:42,538 --> 00:01:46,744
free stops, transit hints, and links to real BAYLINK pages.

29
00:01:46,844 --> 00:01:52,000
The dashboard answers graph questions, like free events by neighborhood,

30
00:01:52,100 --> 00:01:57,183
the transit hubs with the most attractions, and family events per line.

31
00:01:57,283 --> 00:02:00,979
And the Cypher log shows every query the agents ran.

32
00:02:01,679 --> 00:02:04,306
This is the same run inside the Band room.

33
00:02:04,406 --> 00:02:08,982
Scout hands off to Planner, Planner to Checker, and here Checker rejects

34
00:02:09,082 --> 00:02:11,904
round one, mentioning Planner with the fixes.

35
00:02:12,004 --> 00:02:15,346
Planner answers with round two, and Checker approves.

36
00:02:15,446 --> 00:02:18,853
Thoughts and tool calls are posted as room events too.

37
00:02:19,553 --> 00:02:23,237
In the code, the Checker re-queries every stop in Neo4j:

38
00:02:23,337 --> 00:02:25,940
the date, free or paid, the minimum age.

39
00:02:26,040 --> 00:02:30,399
Then it asks a second Crusoe model for the fit, and if anything is

40
00:02:30,499 --> 00:02:33,305
wrong, it sends the plan back through Band.

41
00:02:33,405 --> 00:02:38,034
And each agent only acts on messages that Band delivers to it, so Band

42
00:02:38,134 --> 00:02:40,264
really is the coordination layer.

43
00:02:40,964 --> 00:02:42,245
BAYBAY Crew.

44
00:02:42,345 --> 00:02:49,153
Crusoe thinks, Neo4j remembers, Band coordinates. Thank you!

